  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 400 AM CST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nQuiet weather conditions over the Northland this morning, with\nsome mid to high clouds spreading northward over the Brainerd\nLakes region up toward Lake of the Woods. Otherwise, clear skies\nand light winds prevail. Temperatures under the clouds are in\nthe 20s, where clear skies and calm winds have allowed locations\nto drop into the single digits and lower teens. \n\nThe clouds are from an upper low spinning southward through the\nnorthern Plains, with surface high pressure now centered to our\neast sill influencing much of the area. For today, will \ncontinue to see dry conditions with south winds on the west side\nof the surface high, at less than 10mph. Sunny skies will allow\ntemps to warm into the upper 30s to lower 40s today. Similar \nconditions tonight, light winds and mostly clear skies, will \nallow lows to fall into the teens.\n\nThe upper level low over our region will merge with a broader \nseries of troughs in the southeastern states and morph into a \nlarger upper level low. Surface lows and plenty of precip will \naffect the east coast through the later part of the week as a \nresult, and we will essentially be in-between systems in the \nNorthland through the end of the week. Expect a pretty stagnant \npattern with dry weather and temperatures warming up slightly \nthrough the end of the week. \n\nAn atmospheric river along the west coast will eventually spread\nsome moisture our way via a low pressure system that's looking\nto track almost due east just north of the Canadian border. Some\nrain and snow is likely with this, though we may just catch the\nsouthern edge of the precipitation. Among most ensembles, much\nof our region will remain dry or just see very light precipitation\namounts (just a few hundredths of an inch of liquid equivalent \nprecipitation; maybe a few tenths of an inch of snow). In the \nless likely event that the low takes a more southerly track, \nplaces along the International Border could see some slightly \nboosted amounts, but even with that, impacts should be minimal. \nPrecipitation type will likely be dependent on time of day, with\nsnow at night and rain or mixed precipitation during the day \nfor what few places see precipitation this weekend.\n\nEarly next week, the upper level pattern is looking quasi-zonal\nwith potential for some weak upper level troughs to pass\nthrough. There may be some hit-or-miss chances for light rain or\nsnow, but as of now, impacts look pretty minimal and certainly\nno large storms are hinted at in the models at this time.",
